---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Whether an education loan is secured or unsecured depends mostly on how much you borrow.</strong>
    Small loans usually need no collateral (nothing pledged), mid-size loans usually need a guarantor, and large loans usually need property or deposits as security. The loan type is one decision, and the skill portfolio you build alongside the degree is what repays it and moves you toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short answer:</strong> under the Indian Banks' Association model scheme that many banks follow, loans up to ₹4 lakh need no security, loans up to ₹7.5 lakh need a third-party guarantee, and larger loans need tangible collateral. Each bank sets its own rules, so confirm the band in the sanction letter.</li>
      <li><strong>Decision filter:</strong> "unsecured" does not mean nobody is at risk. Your co-borrower, usually a parent, is liable for the full debt, and a secured loan puts the pledged asset on the line as well.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> get one written offer with collateral and one without, compare the rupee gap, and check PM-Vidyalaxmi first if your institution is on its list. A skill you can show is what repays the loan.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Families usually ask this question when an admission letter arrives and a bank officer asks for property papers. That is the moment to slow down, because pledging a home to save half a point of interest is a poor trade, as the numbers below show.
  </p>

  <p class="more-guides">If this loan decision carries real money for your family, <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-college-students/">career counselling for college students</a> is the direct route, and more <a href="/blog/college-degrees/">College Degrees guides</a> sit in the same section.</p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <p class="note-line">General information, not financial advice. Get every term in writing, and never pay an agent who promises a guaranteed loan.</p>

  <h2 id="answer">The short answer: the amount you borrow decides if an education loan is secured or unsecured</h2>

  <p>
    A loan has no fixed label. The bank chooses what to ask for based on the loan size, your institution, your course and who stands behind you.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <strong>secured</strong> loan means you or your family pledge something the bank can sell if repayment fails, such as a house, a fixed deposit or an insurance policy with a surrender value. An <strong>unsecured</strong> loan means nothing is pledged, so the bank relies on your admission, your course and the co-borrower's income and credit record.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Two beliefs that cost families money</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"Unsecured means nobody has to repay if things go wrong." It does not. The co-borrower still owes the full amount.</li>
      <li>"Secured is always the better deal because the rate is lower." Sometimes the saving is small next to the risk to the asset.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bands">The three loan-size bands, and what each one asks for</h2>

  <p>
    The Indian Banks' Association (IBA) model scheme is the pattern many public sector banks follow. The RBI forwarded it to banks in 2001 and said <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=369&Mode=0" rel="noopener" target="_blank">no security may be insisted upon for loans up to ₹4 lakh</a>. A <a href="https://eparlib.sansad.in/bitstream/123456789/591371/1/94990.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Lok Sabha reply</a> listed the three bands below, and a June 2026 report in <a href="https://www.theweek.in/theweek/business/2026/06/20/education-loans-what-you-need-to-know-about-approval-repayment.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">The Week</a> describes the same bands as typical today.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Loan size</th>
          <th>Security usually asked</th>
          <th>Who stands behind the loan</th>
          <th>In plain words</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {bandRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Loan size"><strong>{row.size}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Security usually asked">{row.security}</td>
            <td data-label="Who stands behind the loan">{row.behind}</td>
            <td data-label="In plain words">{row.kind}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">Banks apply their own version of these bands, and some private lenders ignore them. Confirm the band in your sanction letter, not in a verbal promise.</p>

  <h2 id="meaning">What secured and unsecured mean for you in practice</h2>

  <div class="split-grid">
    <div class="split-card split-card--risk">
      <div class="split-card__label">Secured loan</div>
      <ul>
        <li>The bank holds a claim on your asset until the loan is cleared.</li>
        <li>The rate is often a little lower. The Week reported SBI at 9.40% with collateral against 9.90% without, in June 2026.</li>
        <li>Valuation and legal papers add steps and charges, so ask for the full list.</li>
        <li>If repayment fails, the bank can act against the asset.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="split-card split-card--grow">
      <div class="split-card__label">Unsecured loan</div>
      <ul>
        <li>No asset is pledged. The bank judges your admission, institution, course and the co-borrower.</li>
        <li>The rate is often higher, and NBFCs (non-bank lenders) can sanction larger unsecured amounts at a higher cost.</li>
        <li>The co-borrower's credit score matters. Lenders generally prefer a score near 750 or above.</li>
        <li>If repayment fails, no asset is seized, but the lender can still recover the debt.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>Your parent is not a bystander on an unsecured loan. A co-borrower is jointly liable from day one, and the bank can ask them for the full balance without chasing you first.</p>
    <p>A guarantor is liable only if the borrowers default, yet the loan still shows on their credit report. For a lender comparison built around study abroad, read <a href="/blog/study-abroad/education-loan-for-study-abroad-india/">education loan for study abroad India</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="no-collateral">Large loans that are still collateral-free</h2>

  <p>
    The ₹7.5 lakh line is not a wall. Government-backed routes let some students borrow more without pledging property.
  </p>

  <div class="scheme-grid">
    <div class="scheme-card">
      <h3>PM-Vidyalaxmi</h3>
      <p>Students admitted on merit to the 1,425 institutions on the scheme list get loans with no collateral and no third-party guarantor. There is no upper cap on the amount, which follows fees and related costs. Interest is capped at the bank's benchmark rate plus 0.5%, and repayment can run up to 15 years after the moratorium, which is the course plus one year with no monthly instalment (EMI) to pay.</p>
      <p class="scheme-row scheme-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Management quota and NRI quota seats are excluded. In 2025-26, 70,852 of 110,667 applications on the portal were sanctioned, so keep a backup plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scheme-card">
      <h3>Interest help</h3>
      <p>This lowers your cost, not the collateral rule. PM-Vidyalaxmi gives 3% interest subvention (the government pays that share of the interest) during the moratorium on loans up to ₹10 lakh when family income is below ₹8 lakh. A central scheme gives full interest subvention for technical and professional courses when family income is up to ₹4.5 lakh.</p>
      <p class="scheme-row scheme-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Only 1 lakh fresh slots a year exist for the 3% benefit, and it cannot be combined with another subsidy or scholarship.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scheme-card">
      <h3>Credit guarantee</h3>
      <p>The Credit Guarantee Fund Scheme for Education Loans, set up in 2015, backs 75% of a collateral-free loan up to ₹7.5 lakh. It is meant for students who cannot arrange collateral or a guarantor, with no family income ceiling.</p>
      <p class="scheme-row scheme-row--teal"><span>Best for</span>Students who cannot offer collateral or a guarantor and need a loan up to ₹7.5 lakh.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The details come from the <a href="https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2026/jul/doc2026729936401.pdf" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Government of India PM-Vidyalaxmi document of July 2026</a> and a <a href="https://www.businesstoday.in/amp/education/story/collateral-free-loans-interest-subsidies-centre-puts-rs-1050-crore-into-education-loan-schemes-546001-2026-07-30" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Business Today report</a>. Apply through a bank or the government's <a href="https://www.vidyalakshmi.co.in/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Vidya Lakshmi loan portal</a>, and confirm the current list and limits there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">Does a secured loan cost less? A rupee check</h2>

  <p>
    SBI's rates, as reported by The Week in June 2026, were 9.40% with collateral and 9.90% without. That is a gap of half a point, and rates move, so treat it as a starting point.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p><strong>An illustration, not a real offer.</strong> On ₹10 lakh repaid over ten years, 9.40% gives an EMI near ₹12,885 and about ₹5.46 lakh in total interest. At 9.90%, the EMI is near ₹13,160 and total interest is about ₹5.79 lakh.</p>
    <p>The gap is about ₹33,000 over the whole repayment, before counting interest during the moratorium. That is the price of keeping the family home out of the contract. Test it against your own written quotes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>The type of loan matters less than its size. The safest education loan is the smallest one that closes the real gap, taken for a course whose placement median you have checked.</p>
    <p>If the only way to afford a college is to pledge the family home, compare a lower-cost college and a stronger skill route first.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="default">What happens if repayment fails</h2>

  <div class="ladder">
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">1</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Missed instalments</div>
        <p>Late charges apply and your credit record starts to suffer. The co-borrower's record is affected too.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">2</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Overdue for 90 days</div>
        <p>The account is classed as non-performing, and the bank sends notices to you and every co-borrower or guarantor.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">3</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Secured loan: the asset is at risk</div>
        <p>The bank can move against the pledged property or deposit, usually under the SARFAESI Act, which lets lenders act on secured assets after notice without first going to court. Your loan agreement spells out the steps.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step">
      <div class="ladder-step__num">4</div>
      <div>
        <div class="ladder-step__title">Unsecured loan: no asset, still a debt</div>
        <p>There is nothing to seize, but the lender can pursue recovery through legal action, and the co-borrower remains liable.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Talk to the lender before the account turns overdue. Many banks will discuss a longer tenure or a short pause if you ask early, though the options depend on the bank.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ask">What to ask the bank before you sign</h2>

  <div class="ask-list">
    {askItems.map((item, index) => (
      <div class="ask-item">
        <div class="ask-item__num">{index + 1}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="ask-item__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money check before you pay for a degree with a loan</h2>

  <p>
    Our strongest suggestion: keep a course or degree fee near a tenth of the family education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a caution that needs course-specific evidence. This does not apply to very cheap online courses. Free YouTube lessons and low-priced courses on sites like Udemy or Coursera need little budget care, and the same course sold as a university certificate can cost 30 to 100 times more.
  </p>

  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 3 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 30,000 for the main course and a fifth is Rs 60,000. Spend the rest on tools, practice and a reserve.
  </p>

  <p>
    A loan that needs the family home as security is a fresh reason to stop and compare. Check a lower-cost college and a skill-first route before you sign, and keep money aside for internet, tools and projects that build proof of work. <strong>A high-income skill portfolio, built alongside the degree, is what repays the loan.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Check before you commit: the four questions and the three proofs</h2>

  <p>
    Answer the <strong>four questions</strong> first, then pass the <strong>three proofs</strong>. Together they show whether this loan is a decision or a hope.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol">
    {checkpointItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="protocol-row">
        <div class="protocol-row__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="protocol-row__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Proof 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Show it</span>
        <p>Make a one-page repayment sheet: loan amount, rate, moratorium interest, EMI and the starting pay needed. Add one small piece of work in your field that you could show an employer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Proof 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Say it</span>
        <p>Explain to your parents in 30 seconds to 2 minutes, without notes, what is pledged, who is liable and what the EMI is.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Proof 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Get it checked</span>
        <p>Show the sheet to a bank officer, a recent graduate of the same course and someone who has repaid an education loan. Ask what they would check twice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive mistakes with education loans</h2>

  <div class="mistake-list">
    {mistakeItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="mistake__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div>
          <div class="mistake__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Find your loan size, match it to the band in the table, and ask the bank to confirm the security in writing. Then request one secured and one unsecured quote and compare the rupee gap over the full repayment.
  </p>

  <p>
    A specialist can look at your course, your family's finances and your skill plan together. A sensible first step is <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-college-students/">career counselling for college students</a>. The aim is the decisions that unlock high income opportunities and an earlier path to financial freedom.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read also <a href="/blog/college-degrees/education-loan-worth-it-india-private-college/">education loan worth it for a private college</a>, <a href="/blog/college-degrees/private-college-vs-government-college-career-outcomes-india/">private college vs government college career outcomes</a>, <a href="/blog/college-degrees/how-to-read-college-placement-report-india/">how to read a college placement report</a> and <a href="/blog/college-degrees/scholarships-for-sc-and-st-students-for-professional-and-postgraduate-courses-india/">scholarships for professional and postgraduate courses</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQs on whether an education loan is secured or unsecured</h2>

  <div class="faq-accordion">
    {faqItems.map((item) => (
      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>{item.q}</summary>
        <div class="faq-a">{item.a}</div>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

  <style>
